====================================================================================================
(A) THE COSET IDENTITY AND ITS FOURIER PROOF
====================================================================================================
  product form, v = 1..60, 200 random t each      : worst |LHS-RHS| = 2.524e-16
  Fourier mechanism: the coset average annihilates every harmonic with v not dividing n,
  so the two partial sums below are EQUAL TERM BY TERM, not merely close.
  Fourier partial sums to n = 3000, 10 values of v : worst |A-B| = 8.793e-14 (at v = 1)
  (A) VERDICT: identity and its Fourier proof both confirmed

====================================================================================================
(B) q = 2, WITH A PROOF AND A CONSTANT.  arc average G(L) = log2 + Cl_2(2pi{L})/(2pi L)
====================================================================================================
  Cl_2(pi/3) = 1.0149416064   (the maximum of the Clausen function on (0, 2 pi))
  MY FALSIFIED PREDICTION  log2 - 3 Cl_2(pi/3)/(5 pi) = 0.4993076122  (claimed at L = 5/6;
  wrong, because L is in the denominator too -- the extremiser of Cl_2 is not the
  extremiser of Cl_2(2 pi L)/L.  Kept in the log on purpose.)
  half log 2                                  = 0.3465735903   <- what the theorem needs
  target with the margin, (1/2) log2 + delta   = 0.4620981204
  TRUE minimum over L in (0,6]:  0.494530 at L = 0.790827   (measured, not proved; clears (1/2)log2 + delta by 0.032432)
         L    Clausen form   direct quadrature       diff
    0.2500      1.27626899          1.27628337   1.44e-05
    0.5000      0.69314718          0.69316243   1.53e-05
    0.8333      0.49930761          0.49932350   1.59e-05
    1.0000      0.69314718          0.69319211   4.49e-05
    3.0000      0.69314718          0.69319073   4.36e-05

  THE BOUND THAT IS PROVABLE BY HAND (this is what goes in the paper):
    L <= 1/2 (convexity, midpoint):  G >= -log sin(pi L/2) >= -log sin(pi/4) = 0.346574 = (1/2) log 2
       midpoint bound valid at every L (min over L of G - midpoint bound) : 3.069e-01  (must be >= 0)
       at the endpoint L = 1/2 the two-point midpoint bound gives          : 0.519860  (so the inequality is strict there)
    L >= 1/2 (Clausen, 1/(2 pi L) <= 1/pi):  G >= log2 - Cl_2(pi/3)/pi     = 0.370081
  (B) VERDICT: both branches clear (1/2) log 2, so q = 2 has a proof
  NOTE what the theorem actually needs: mean >= (1/2) log2 - o(1) at EVERY minor theta.
  The delta margin exists only so Hoeffding can take a bite, and the bite can be taken
  as o(1) instead -- so q = 1 and q = 2 do not need the margin, only (1/2) log 2.

